  "dates are valid <code>\"DD/MM\"</code> strings with leading zeros"
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(teams, matches):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What do you need per team before you can rank?",
   "a": "Four numbers: points, goals scored, alternate points, and the registration date. Matches only feed the first three. Ranking is then a sort inside each group."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "Team X: one 2-0 win and one 0-1 loss. What are its numbers?",
   "a": "Points 3 + 0 = 3. Goals 2 + 0 = 2. Alternate points 5 + 1 = 6. Note the goals <b>conceded</b> do not count anywhere."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What would you write first?",
   "a": "For every comparison during the sort, rescan all matches to get both teams' numbers. That is O(T &times; M) work or worse. Another trap: comparing dates as strings, so <code>\"30/01\"</code> sorts after <code>\"02/02\"</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Clue words?",
   "a": "\"rank by A, then B, then C\" &rarr; <b>sort with a tuple key</b>. Python compares tuples left to right, which is exactly a chain of tie-breakers. Negate the \"higher is better\" numbers so one ascending sort works. Aggregate first with a <b>dict</b> keyed by team."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li><code>stats = {team: [0, 0, 0]}</code>.</li><li>One pass over matches: add goals; give win/draw/loss points and alternate points.</li><li>Group the teams; key each one by <code>(-pts, -goals, -alt, month, day)</code>.</li><li>Sort each group and return the names.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "No matches at all (ranking by date only). Dates in different months where string order is wrong. A 0-0 draw (both get 1 point and 3 alternate points). A group with one team. Two groups whose results must not mix."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "Do one pass over the matches and keep <code>[points, goals, alt_points]</code> per team in a dict. A loss still adds 1 alternate point.",
  "Sort with a tuple key. Use negatives for \"more is better\": <code>(-pts, -goals, -alt, month, day)</code>.",
  "Parse the date: <code>day, month = date.split(\"/\")</code>, then compare <code>(int(month), int(day))</code>, never the raw string. Group with <code>defaultdict(list)</code> keyed by group."
 ],
 "solution": "from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solution(teams, matches):\n    # per team: [points, goals scored, alternate points]\n    stats = {name: [0, 0, 0] for name, _date, _group in teams}\n    for a, b, ga, gb in matches:\n        stats[a][1] += ga\n        stats[b][1] += gb\n        if ga > gb:\n            win, lose = a, b\n        elif gb > ga:\n            win, lose = b, a\n        else:                                 # draw: 1 point, 3 alternate points each\n            for t in (a, b):\n                stats[t][0] += 1\n                stats[t][2] += 3\n            continue\n        stats[win][0] += 3\n        stats[win][2] += 5\n        stats[lose][2] += 1                   # a loss still earns 1 alternate point\n\n    groups = defaultdict(list)\n    for name, date, group in teams:\n        day, month = date.split('/')\n        pts, goals, alt = stats[name]\n        # negate the \"higher is better\" keys; (month, day) earlier is better\n        key = (-pts, -goals, -alt, int(month), int(day))\n        groups[group].append((key, name))\n    return {g: [name for _key, name in sorted(rows)] for g, rows in sorted(groups.items())}\n",
 "brute": "def solution(teams, matches):\n    # Recompute each team's numbers by scanning every match, then a selection sort\n    def numbers(name):\n        pts = goals = alt = 0\n        for a, b, ga, gb in matches:\n            if name == a:\n                mine, theirs = ga, gb\n            elif name == b:\n                mine, theirs = gb, ga\n            else:\n                continue\n            goals += mine\n            if mine > theirs:\n                pts += 3; alt += 5\n            elif mine == theirs:\n                pts += 1; alt += 3\n            else:\n                alt += 1\n        return pts, goals, alt\n\n    def better(x, y):\n        # True if team row x ranks above team row y\n        px, py = numbers(x[0]), numbers(y[0])\n        if px != py:\n            return px > py                     # tuple compare: points, goals, alt\n        dx, mx = x[1].split('/'); dy, my = y[1].split('/')\n        return (int(mx), int(dx)) < (int(my), int(dy))\n\n    result = {}\n    for g in sorted(set(t[2] for t in teams)):\n        rows = [t for t in teams if t[2] == g]\n        ordered = []\n        while rows:\n            best = rows[0]\n            for r in rows[1:]:\n                if better(r, best):\n                    best = r\n            ordered.append(best[0])\n            rows.remove(best)\n        result[g] = ordered\n    return result\n",
 "explain": "<p>Example 1:</p><ul><li>firstTeam 0-3 secondTeam: secondTeam +3 points, +5 alt; firstTeam +1 alt.</li><li>secondTeam 1-1 thirdTeam: both +1 point, +3 alt.</li><li>thirdTeam 2-0 firstTeam: thirdTeam +3 points, +5 alt; firstTeam +1 alt.</li><li>Totals (points, goals, alt): secondTeam (4, 4, 8), thirdTeam (4, 3, 8), firstTeam (0, 0, 2).</li><li>Keys: <code>(-4, -4, -8, 6, 4)</code> &lt; <code>(-4, -3, -8, 4, 24)</code> &lt; <code>(0, 0, -2, 5, 17)</code>.</li></ul><p>The tuple key makes the rule list and the code line up one to one. When the interviewer adds a fifth tie-breaker, it is one more element in the tuple, not another nested <code>if</code>.</p>",
 "complexity": "O(M + T log T) time for M matches and T teams; O(T) extra space",
